'''Seinäjoen Jalkapallokerho''', commonly referred to as '''SJK''', also internationally '''SJK Seinäjoki''', is a [[Finland|Finnish]] professional [[association football|football]] club from the city of [[Seinäjoki]]. The club plays in the [[Veikkausliiga]], the highest tier of the Finnish league system. Their home ground is [[OmaSP Stadion]], which is located near the city center and next to SJK's training facility Wallsport. SJK was formed in 2007 after the merger of [[TP-Seinäjoki]] and [[Sepsi-78]]. After starting in the third-tier, the club won the promotion to Veikkausliiga in 2014, and the next year won its first [[2015 Veikkausliiga|Finnish championship title]].

==History==

===The early years===
SJK was formed on 5 November 2007 as a merger between the first teams of [[TP-Seinäjoki]] and [[Sepsi-78]]. It was told to the public five days later. [[Sepsi-78]] had a board meeting on 24 October and [[TP-Seinäjoki]] on the next day. Both clubs supported the merger.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.tp-seinajoki.fi/uutisarkisto/115_1565.html |author= TP-Seinäjoki |title=Kaupunkijoukkueasia hyväksyttiin|access-date= 26 January 2008}} {{dead link|date=October 2020}}</ref> Sepsi-78 and TP both gave up their place in [[Kakkonen]].<ref>{{cite web|url= http://www.ilkka.fi/arc_article.jsp?article=295199& | author = Ilkka | title = Sepsi-78:lle ja TP-Seinäjoelle yhteinen edustusjoukkue | access-date = 27 January 2008}} {{dead link|date=October 2020}}</ref> The first signing of the new club was Petri Huttu.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://sjk.fi/uutiset/pete-ehti-ensimmaisena/ | author = SJK | title = Pete ehti ensimmäisenä | date = 26 January 2008 | access-date = 26 January 2008 | archive-date = 11 October 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201011030313/https://sjk.fi/uutiset/pete-ehti-ensimmaisena/ | url-status = live }}</ref> There were several negotiations about the merger over the years. The main reason was the inadequacy of players. The clubs continued doing junior work after the merger, but could also have their own team in the lower divisions. The work name of the club was Seinäjoki Futis, and there was a contest about the official name, where everyone could suggest a name. The final name was Seinäjoen Jalkapallokerho, that had been suggested by many people. There was also a contest about the logo and uniform.<ref name="multiple1">{{cite web| url=http://www.tp-seinajoki.fi/uutisarkisto/115_1552.html | author = TP-Seinäjoki | title=Sepsi ja TP yhdistävät edustusjoukkueensa| access-date = 26 January 2008}} {{dead link|date=October 2020}}</ref> The registered name of the club is Seinäjoen Jalkapallokerho ry, and it was registered on January 22, 2008.<ref>{{cite web| url=http://yhdistysrekisteri.prh.fi/ryhaku.htx?kieli=1&hakuraja=0&nimi=Sein%E4joen+Jalkapallokerho+ry&kotipaikka=Sein%E4joki&ensrek=&viimrek=&osoite=&evlu=&orto=&vapaa=&sb_haku=Hae | author = Yhdistysrekisteri | title =Yhdistysrekisteri| access-date = 30 April 2008}} {{dead link|date=October 2020}}</ref> Mikko Latomäki was chosen as the first chairman. Jukka Saarijärvi was chosen as the vice chairman. Saarijärvi was a former TP-Seinäjoki chairman.

===Promotion to Veikkausliiga===
====2012–2016: Simo Valakari era====
In 2013 SJK started its second season in [[Ykkönen]]. In 2012 SJK had been placed second. SJK made some signings for 2013, which included a Spanish [[La Masia]]  graduate [[Josu]] and Estonian [[Mihkel Aksalu]]. SJK started the season on May 4 with a 2–1 win over [[PK-35 Vantaa (men)|PK-35 Vantaa]]. SJK played [[AC Kajaani]] on June 30 and won 1–7. Kajaani made a complaint to [[Palloliitto]] about the amount of foreigners in SJK. The complaint went through and AC Kajaani was changed as the winner of the match. SJK complained about the decision and the result was again changed. AC Kajaani complained again, but later canceled it.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://yle.fi/uutiset/ac_kajaani_peruu_sjk-valituksensa/6846009 | title = AC Kajaani peruu SJK-valituksensa | author = Yle.fi | date = 24 September 2013 | access-date = 3 October 2013 | archive-date = 5 October 2013 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20131005050507/http://yle.fi/uutiset/ac_kajaani_peruu_sjk-valituksensa/6846009 | url-status = live }}</ref> After that SJK lost five matches in a row. SJK however did win eight matches in a row after that. The promotion to [[Veikkausliiga]] was confirmed on 29 September 2013 in a homematch against [[FC Haka]]. There was a record breaking attendance (4 798). The match was a 1–1 draw.

SJK started the season 2014 with winning the [[Finnish League Cup|League Cup]]. SJK won the final 2–0 against local rivals [[Vaasan Palloseura]].<ref>{{cite web | url = http://yle.fi/urheilu/nousijajoukkue_valloitti_liigacupin_pohjanmaan_derbyssa/7163088 | title = Nousijajoukkue valloitti Liigacupin Pohjanmaan derbyssä | author = Yle Urheilu | date = 29 March 2014 | access-date = 29 March 2014 | archive-date = 31 March 2014 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20140331052232/http://yle.fi/urheilu/nousijajoukkue_valloitti_liigacupin_pohjanmaan_derbyssa/7163088 | url-status = live }}</ref> The season started poorly, at the end of May the club was at the eleventh place. After that SJK won seven games in a row and those results lifted SJK to the second place. [[Helsingin Jalkapalloklubi|HJK]] won the league and SJK was second. SJK was the only club to win against HJK under [[Mika Lehkosuo]]. SJK lost only two of the last 24 games. The most important players were [[Mihkel Aksalu]], the league's best defender [[Cedric Gogoua]], [[Johannes Laaksonen]] and [[Toni Lehtinen]].<ref name="urheilumuseo">{{cite web | url = http://www.urheilumuseo.fi/portals/47/veikkausliiga/seurat/sjk.htm | title = Veikkausliigan verkkonäyttely: Seinäjoen Jalkapallokerho (SJK) | author = Urheilumuseo.fi | access-date = 16 November 2015 | archive-date = 11 April 2016 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160411001219/http://urheilumuseo.fi/portals/47/veikkausliiga/seurat/sjk.htm | url-status = dead }}</ref>

For the 2015 season SJK got even stronger. There were new signings like [[Mehmet Hetemaj]] and [[Ariel Ngueukam]]. SJK was considered as the biggest challenger of HJK. SJK was at the top of the table in September. SJK won HJK 3–0 in [[Seinäjoki]]. At the end of the season SJK's biggest challenger was [[Rovaniemen Palloseura]]. SJK won the league with one point difference to RoPS. The most important players were [[Roope Riski]] and [[Liverpool FC]] loanee [[Allan (footballer, born 1997)|Allan]]. SJK was chosen as the team of the year in Finland's sports gala.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.hs.fi/urheilu/a1452578710578 | title = Mäkäräisen nousu sykähdyttävin urheiluhetki, Petteri Piironen vuoden valmentaja – lue tästä, ketkä kaikki palkittiin Urheilugaalassa | author = hs.fi | access-date = 16 January 2016 | archive-date = 15 January 2016 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20160115093224/http://www.hs.fi/urheilu/a1452578710578 | url-status = dead }}</ref>

SJK won the [[Finnish Cup]] in 2016. It was the first time in the club's history.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://yle.fi/urheilu/3-9190005|title=SJK kukisti HJK:n Suomen Cupin finaalissa – Hetemaj ratkaisi rankkarikisan|author=yle.fi|date=24 September 2016|access-date=2016-09-24|archive-date=2016-09-27|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160927201057/http://yle.fi/urheilu/3-9190005|url-status=live}}</ref>

====2017–present====
SJK's head coach [[Simo Valakari]] was surprisingly sacked on February 17, 2017. He was replaced by [[Sixten Boström]].<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.sjk2007.fi/w/simo-valakari-on-vapautettu-sjk-n-paavalmentajavastuusta | title = Simo Valakari on vapautettu SJK:n päävalmentajavastuusta | author = SJK | access-date = 17 February 2017 | archive-date = 24 August 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170824135507/http://www.sjk2007.fi/w/simo-valakari-on-vapautettu-sjk-n-paavalmentajavastuusta | url-status = dead }}</ref> Sixten Boström got also sacked after poor results in May and was replaced by Spanish [[José Manuel Roca]]<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.iltalehti.fi/jalkapallo/a/201706022200180844|title=Kommentti: José Manuel Roca on SJK:n kolmas valmentaja tänä vuonna|access-date=2017-06-02|archive-date=2017-06-02|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170602101724/http://www.iltalehti.fi/jalkapallo/201706022200180844_vg.shtml|url-status=live}}</ref> Roca was sacked in September. [[SJK Akatemia]] coaches Brian Page and Toni Lehtinen did the job for the rest of the poor season.<ref>[https://www.sjk.fi/w/brian-page-ja-toni-lehtinen-vetavat-kauden-loppuun Brian Page ja Toni Lehtinen vetavat kauden loppuun.] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190825213931/http://www.sjk.fi/w/brian-page-ja-toni-lehtinen-vetavat-kauden-loppuun |date=2019-08-25 }} SJK</ref> Under Page and Lehtinen SJK lost the [[Finnish Cup]] final to HJK.<ref>[https://www.palloliitto.fi/videot/jalkapallosarjat/kooste-respect-suomen-cup-finaali-2017 Suomen cup-finaali-2017.] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171207135824/https://www.palloliitto.fi/videot/jalkapallosarjat/kooste-respect-suomen-cup-finaali-2017 |date=2017-12-07 }} Palloliitto</ref> [[Tommi Kautonen]] was hired as the new head coach in October 2017.<ref>[https://www.sjk.fi/w/tommi-kautonen-sjk-n-paavalmentaja-kaudesta-2018 Tommi Kautonen SJK:n päävalmentaja kaudesta 2018.] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180808104831/https://www.sjk.fi/w/tommi-kautonen-sjk-n-paavalmentaja-kaudesta-2018 |date=2018-08-08 }} SJK</ref> The season 2018 didn't start as planned and Kautonen was sacked. He was replaced by [[Aleksei Borisovich Yeryomenko]]. Yeryomenko could save SJK from relegation.<ref>{{cite web| url = http://www.sjk.fi/w/alexei-eremenko-sr--korvaa-tommi-kautosen| title = Alexei Eremenko sr. korvaa Tommi Kautosen| author = Sjk.fi| date = 22 May 2018| access-date = 22 May 2018| archive-date = 23 May 2018| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180523010926/http://www.sjk.fi/w/alexei-eremenko-sr--korvaa-tommi-kautosen| url-status = live}}</ref> Yeryomenko continued as the head coach in 2019. SJK signed for example [[Sergei Eremenko]] and [[Moshtagh Yaghoubi]].<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.sjk.fi/w/tervetuloa-sergei-eremenko| title = Tervetuloa Sergei Eremenko| author = Sjk.fi| date = 25 January 2019| access-date = 28 December 2019| archive-date = 28 December 2019| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191228125347/https://www.sjk.fi/w/tervetuloa-sergei-eremenko| url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.sjk.fi/w/tervetuloa-kerhoon-mosa-| title = Tervetuloa Kerhoon Mosa| author = Sjk.fi| date = 31 January 2019| access-date = 28 December 2019| archive-date = 28 December 2019| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191228125344/https://www.sjk.fi/w/tervetuloa-kerhoon-mosa-| url-status = live}}</ref> SJK didn't win a single game in the group stage of the Finnish Cup in the winter.<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.sjk.fi/w/cup-taival-paattyi-kotitasuriin| title = Cup-taival päättyi kotitasuriin| author = Sjk.fi| date = 22 February 2019| access-date = 28 December 2019| archive-date = 28 December 2019| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191228125343/https://www.sjk.fi/w/cup-taival-paattyi-kotitasuriin| url-status = live}}</ref> The Veikkausliiga-campaign started well, because SJK won in the first round for the first time ever.<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.sjk.fi/w/veikkausliiga-kayntiin-komeasti-voitolla| title = Veikkausliiga käyntiin komeasti voitolla| author = Sjk.fi| date = 6 April 2019| access-date = 28 December 2019| archive-date = 28 December 2019| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191228125347/https://www.sjk.fi/w/veikkausliiga-kayntiin-komeasti-voitolla| url-status = live}}</ref> SJK won [[Kokkolan Pallo-Veikot|KPV]] in the end of April and was at the first place of the league.<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.sjk.fi/w/sjk-nousi-voitolla-veikkausliigan-karkeen2| title = SJK nousi voitolla Veikkausliigan kärkeen| author = Sjk.fi| access-date = 28 December 2019| archive-date = 28 December 2019| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191228125344/https://www.sjk.fi/w/sjk-nousi-voitolla-veikkausliigan-karkeen2| url-status = dead}}</ref> Despite the good start, SJK couldn't get good results. Yeryomenko was sacked in August. The replacement was Brian Page.<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.sjk.fi/w/sjk-n-ja-alexei-eremenkon-tiet-erkanevat| title = SJK:n ja Alexei Eremenkon tiet erkanevat| author = Sjk.fi| date = 16 August 2019| access-date = 28 December 2019| archive-date = 23 October 2019| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191023112047/https://www.sjk.fi/w/sjk-n-ja-alexei-eremenkon-tiet-erkanevat| url-status = live}}</ref> SJK didn't win any games for the rest of the season. SJK placed ninth.

After the season SJK appointed [[Jani Honkavaara]] as the new head coach.<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.sjk.fi/w/jani-honkavaara-on-sjk-n-uusi-paavalmentaja| title = Jani Honkavaara on SJK:n uusi päävalmentaja| author = Sjk.fi| date = 23 October 2019| access-date = 28 December 2019| archive-date = 28 December 2019| archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20191228125344/https://www.sjk.fi/w/jani-honkavaara-on-sjk-n-uusi-paavalmentaja| url-status = live}}</ref> Honkavaara signed many prolific players for the [[2020 SJK season|2020 season]], such as [[Ariel Ngueukam]], [[Robin Sid]], [[Tero Mäntylä]] and [[Niko Markkula]].<ref name="edustus">{{cite web | url = http://www.sjk2007.fi/w/edustus | title = Edustus | author = Seinäjoen Jalkapallokerho | access-date = 28 December 2019 | archive-date = 14 March 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170314082925/http://www.sjk2007.fi/w/edustus | url-status = dead }}</ref> In the winter, SJK once again could not get through from the group stage of the Finnish Cup.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sjk.fi/uutiset/niukka-tappio-suomen-mestarin-kotihallissa/|title=Niukka tappio Suomen mestarin kotihallissa|author=Sjk.fi|date=14 February 2020|access-date=29 December 2020|archive-date=20 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210120073050/https://sjk.fi/uutiset/niukka-tappio-suomen-mestarin-kotihallissa/|url-status=live}}</ref> As the [[Veikkausliiga]] season started in July, SJK won in the first round against [[Turun Palloseura|TPS]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sjk.fi/uutiset/kausi-kayntiin-voitolla-omasp-stadionilla/|title=Kausi käyntiin voitolla OmaSP Stadionilla|author=Sjk.fi|date=July 2020|access-date=29 December 2020|archive-date=19 December 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201219205513/https://sjk.fi/uutiset/kausi-kayntiin-voitolla-omasp-stadionilla/|url-status=live}}</ref> SJK had a good start to the season, but the results started to get worse quite quickly.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sjk.fi/uutiset/rikkonainen-ottelu-paattyi-viime-sekuntien-tappioon/|title=Rikkonainen ottelu päättyi viime sekuntien tappioon|author=Sjk.fi|date=22 July 2020|access-date=29 December 2020|archive-date=13 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210113162636/https://sjk.fi/uutiset/rikkonainen-ottelu-paattyi-viime-sekuntien-tappioon/|url-status=live}}</ref> In the summer [[transfer window]] the club signed a couple of players, such as [[Jake Jervis]] and [[Emmanuel Ledesma]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sjk.fi/uutiset/sjkn-hyokkays-vahvistuu-kahdella-pelaajalla/|title=SJK:n hyökkäys vahvistuu kahdella pelaajalla|author=Sjk.fi|date=19 August 2020|access-date=29 December 2020|archive-date=28 October 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201028060456/https://sjk.fi/uutiset/sjkn-hyokkays-vahvistuu-kahdella-pelaajalla/|url-status=live}}</ref> They proved themselves quickly and with their lead SJK could end the season somewhat brightly. SJK finished seventh, which was disappointing for the club and the supporters.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sjk.fi/uutiset/kausi-paattyi-dramattiseen-voittoon-rovaniemella/|title=Kausi päättyi dramaattiseen voittoon Rovaniemellä|date=4 November 2020|access-date=29 December 2020|archive-date=24 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201124170446/https://sjk.fi/uutiset/kausi-paattyi-dramattiseen-voittoon-rovaniemella/|url-status=live}}</ref> In the [[2021 Veikkausliiga|2021 season]], Honkavaara led SJK to finish 3rd in the league and win bronze medals.

During the [[2022 Veikkausliiga|2022]] and [[2023 Veikkausliiga|2023]] seasons, the first team was managed by [[Joaquín Gómez (football manager) |Joaquín Gómez]]. In August 2022, club's academy product [[Daniel Håkans]] was loaned out to [[FK Jerv]] in Norwegian [[Eliteserien|top-tier]], and later acquired by Jerv on a permanent contract.

On 6 March 2023, SJK officially announced a co-operation deal with [[Ghana|Ghanaian]] football academy [[Vision F.C.|Vision FC]], presented by the sporting director [[Richie Dorman]]. SJK and Vision had already worked together for a while before the announcement.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://sjk.fi/uutiset/ghanalainen-vision-fc-sjkn-yhteistyoseuraksi/|title=Ghanalainen Vision FC SJK:n yhteistyöseuraksi|date=6 March 2023|access-date=3 October 2023}}</ref> After the 2023 season, [[Terry Yegbe]], who had arrived to SJK from Vision, was acquired by [[IF Elfsborg]] for a reported transfer fee of €600.000–700.000.<ref>[https://www.mtvuutiset.fi/artikkeli/mtv-urheilun-tiedot-sjk-komeetta-siirtyy-ruotsin-huippuseuraan/8832116#gs.cldfxq MTV Urheilun tiedot: SJK-komeetta siirtyy Ruotsin huippuseuraan], mtvuutiset.fi, [[MTV Oy]], 3 December 2023</ref>

For the [[2024 Veikkausliiga]] season, the reserve team head coach [[Stevie Grieve]] was appointed the manager of the first team.<ref>[https://www.veikkausliiga.com/uutiset/2023/12/04/stevie-grieve-tarttuu-sjkn-paavalmentajan-tehtaviin-suoriutui-viime-kaudella-ilmiomaisesti Stevie Grieve tarttuu SJK:n päävalmentajan tehtäviin - "Suoriutui viime kaudella ilmiömäisesti"], veikkausliiga.com, 4 December 2023</ref> However, due to his lack of required UEFA Pro-license, he worked first as an assistant coach to [[Toni Lehtinen]]. On 29 May 2024, he was named the manager officially, after he was earlier accepted to UEFA Pro-class.<ref>[https://yle.fi/a/74-20091063 SJK vaihtoi päävalmentajan paikalle aidon päävalmentajansa, kun Skotlannista tuli mieluisia uutisia], [[Yle]], 29 May 2024</ref>

